    "title": "Top 20 Chart-Based Trading Strategies for Gold (XAU/USD)",
    "summary": "An overview of the most popular and proven chart-based strategies used by experienced gold traders — your roadmap for what's coming in this module.",
    "body": "# Top 20 Chart-Based Trading Strategies for Gold (XAU/USD)\n\nGold (XAU/USD) is one of the most actively traded instruments in the world. Its volatility, liquidity, and sensitivity to macro events make it a favourite among professional and retail day traders alike. Below is a curated list of the **top 20 chart-based strategies** used by experienced gold traders — from institutional desks to elite retail traders.\n\n> **Important:** Every strategy below is a *process*, not a signal. None of them tell you to \"buy here\" or \"sell there.\" They tell you *where to look*, *what to wait for*, and *how to manage risk*. That's the difference between gambling and trading.\n\n---\n\n## The Top 20 Strategies\n\n| # | Strategy | Core Idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Fibonacci Retracement** | Enter on pullbacks into the 0.5–0.705 golden zone after a strong impulse |\n| 2 | **Breakout & Retest** | Wait for a key level to break, then re-enter on the retest from the other side |\n| 3 | **Liquidity Sweep & Reversal** | Fade aggressive stop hunts beyond key highs/lows |\n| 4 | **Session Open Play** | Trade the directional expansion after the Asian range at London/NY open |\n| 5 | **Support & Resistance Rejection** | Enter on confirmed rejection candles at strong S/R zones |\n| 6 | **Trend Continuation (Impulse-Pullback)** | Re-enter the trend after a clean pullback to structure or a moving average |\n| 7 | **News Spike Fade** | Fade the overreaction after a high-impact news event (NFP, CPI, Fed) |\n| 8 | **Double Top / Double Bottom** | Reversal pattern at key highs/lows with confirmed neckline break |\n| 9 | **Head & Shoulders** | Classic three-peak reversal pattern signalling a trend change |\n| 10 | **Bull/Bear Flag** | Tight consolidation after a strong impulse, breaks in trend direction |\n| 11 | **Rising/Falling Wedge** | Converging price action that typically breaks opposite to the slope |\n| 12 | **Inside Bar Breakout** | Tight range candle (inside bar) breaks out — entry on the break of its high/low |\n| 13 | **Pin Bar Reversal** | Long-wicked rejection candle at a key level signals a sharp reversal |\n| 14 | **Fair Value Gap (FVG) Fill** | Imbalance left by a fast move — price returns to \"fill the gap\" before continuing |\n| 15 | **Order Block Entry** | Institutional candle that started a strong move — re-entry when price returns to it |\n| 16 | **Moving Average Confluence** | Price pulls back to a key MA (e.g. 20, 50, 200 EMA) that aligns with a structure level |\n| 17 | **Asian Range Breakout** | Trade the break of the high or low set during the quiet Asian session |\n| 18 | **VWAP Reversion** | Price deviates far from VWAP — fade the extension back to the mean |\n| 19 | **Trendline Bounce** | Price pulls back to a well-tested ascending/descending trendline and bounces |\n| 20 | **Change of Character (CHoCH)** | First structural break against the trend — early signal of a potential reversal |\n\n---\n\n## How to Use This List\n\nThis is your **roadmap** for the Trading Strategies module. Each strategy above will have its own dedicated lesson where you'll learn:\n\n- 📌 **Context** — when and where the setup applies\n- 📐 **Location** — the exact level or zone to watch\n- 🧠 **Scenario** — the if-then hypothesis\n- ⚡ **Trigger** — what confirms the entry\n- 🛑 **Invalidation** — where the idea is wrong (your stop)\n- 📏 **Position Sizing** — how to size based on risk\n- 🎯 **Target** — where to take profit\n\n---\n\n## What Makes Gold Different?\n\nGold has some unique characteristics that affect how these strategies perform:\n\n- **High volatility** — Gold can move **$20–$50 in a single session**. Zones need to be wider than in forex.\n- **News sensitivity** — CPI, NFP, Fed decisions, and geopolitical events can invalidate any technical setup instantly. Always check the economic calendar before trading.\n- **Session behaviour** — The **London–New York overlap (13:00–17:00 UTC)** is where the largest moves happen. Many strategies work best during this window.\n- **Round numbers matter** — Levels like $3,700, $3,750, $3,800 act as psychological magnets. Always be aware of the nearest round number.\n- **Trending nature** — Gold loves to trend. Trend-continuation strategies (Fib, Impulse-Pullback, Flags) tend to outperform pure reversal plays over a large sample.\n\n---\n\n## The Golden Rule Across All 20 Strategies\n\n> *\"No strategy works without **context**. A pin bar at a random level is noise. A pin bar at a HTF key level, after a liquidity sweep, in the direction of the 4H trend, during London open — that's a setup.\"*\n\nThe more **confluence** (overlapping reasons) you have, the higher the probability of the setup. No strategy works in isolation — they all work best when they **stack together**.\n\n---\n\nUp next: we'll go through each strategy one by one, starting with **Fibonacci Retracement**. 🚀",
