    "title": "Strategy 1: Fibonacci Retracement",
    "summary": "How to use Fibonacci retracement levels to enter trades in the direction of the higher timeframe trend on gold.",
    "body": "# Strategy 1: Fibonacci Retracement\n\n> **The Edge:** After a strong impulse move, price rarely reverses immediately. It pulls back — and Fibonacci levels tell you *where* that pullback is most likely to stall and continue in the original direction.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The Core Idea\n\nFibonacci retracement is one of the most widely used tools among professional gold traders. It is based on the mathematical ratios discovered by Leonardo Fibonacci — ratios that appear naturally in markets because they reflect the collective behaviour of buyers and sellers.\n\nWhen gold makes a **strong directional move** (impulse), smart money doesn't chase it. They wait for price to **retrace** back to a value area before re-entering. That value area is defined by Fibonacci levels — particularly the **0.5 (50%) and 0.705 (70.5%) golden zone**.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Key Fibonacci Levels to Know\n\n| Level | % Retracement | Significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.236 | 23.6% | Shallow — only valid in very strong trends |\n| 0.382 | 38.2% | Common first retracement level |\n| 0.500 | 50.0% | Mid-point — heavily watched by institutions |\n| 0.618 | 61.8% | The \"golden ratio\" — highest probability zone |\n| 0.705 | 70.5% | End of the golden zone — last line before invalidation |\n| 0.786 | 78.6% | Deep retracement — still valid in slow markets |\n\n> ✅ **The Golden Zone = 0.5 to 0.705** — This is your primary area of interest.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Step-by-Step Process (Bearish Example)\n\n### Step 1 — Confirm 4H Bias\n- Look at the **4H chart** of XAU/USD\n- Confirm the structure is **bearish**: Lower Highs (LH) and Lower Lows (LL)\n- This is your **higher timeframe (HTF) directional bias** — you will ONLY look for sells\n\n### Step 2 — Drop to 1H and Find the Impulse\n- On the **1H chart**, identify the most recent **sharp bearish impulse** move\n- Example: Price drops from **$3,800 → $3,740** in 3–4 strong bearish candles\n- This is the move you will draw your Fibonacci on\n\n### Step 3 — Draw the Fibonacci\n- Anchor point 1 (top): **$3,800** (start of the impulse)\n- Anchor point 2 (bottom): **$3,740** (end of the impulse)\n- Your tool will automatically plot the retracement levels upward\n\n### Step 4 — Wait for Price to Retrace INTO the Golden Zone\n- You are now watching for price to **pull back UP** into the 0.5–0.705 zone\n- 50% of $60 move = $30 → **$3,770**\n- 70.5% of $60 move = $42.30 → **$3,782.30**\n- **Golden zone = $3,770 – $3,782**\n- Do NOT enter just because price enters the zone — wait for confirmation\n\n### Step 5 — Look for a Trigger\nOnce price is inside the golden zone, look for ONE of the following triggers:\n- 🕯️ A **bearish engulfing candle** on the 15m or 5m\n- 📌 A **liquidity sweep** above a recent minor high inside the zone\n- 🔻 A **break of structure** to the downside on the 5m (price makes a lower low)\n- 📊 A **rejection wick** touching a round number or prior level inside the zone\n\n### Step 6 — Define Invalidation (Stop Loss)\n- Your stop goes **above the 0.786 level** or above the swing high that started the impulse\n- If price closes above $3,800 (the impulse origin), the setup is invalid — exit immediately\n- Never move your stop to make a losing trade \"work\"\n\n### Step 7 — Set Your Target\n- **Minimum target:** The low of the impulse ($3,740) = 1R minimum\n- **Primary target:** Next structure low or demand zone below = 2R+\n- **Extended target:** If momentum is strong, trail your stop to lock in gains\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Bullish Example (Mirror Image)\n\n| Step | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| 4H Bias | Bullish — Higher Highs, Higher Lows |\n| 1H Impulse | Sharp move UP from $3,700 → $3,760 |\n| Draw Fib | Bottom ($3,700) → Top ($3,760) |\n| Golden Zone | $3,730 – $3,742 (50%–70.5% retracement DOWN) |\n| Wait | Price pulls BACK DOWN into the zone |\n| Trigger | Bullish engulfing / sweep of lows / break of structure up on 5m |\n| Stop | Below $3,700 (impulse origin) |\n| Target | $3,760 and beyond |\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Why It's Wrong | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Entering as soon as price touches the zone | No confirmation = gambling | Wait for a trigger candle or structure break |\n| Drawing Fib on a small, weak move | Fib only works on strong, clean impulses | Only draw on sharp, multi-candle moves |\n| Ignoring the HTF bias | Counter-trend Fibs have lower probability | Always align with 4H direction |\n| Not defining a stop before entering | You have no invalidation = no plan | Stop goes beyond the 0.786 or swing high/low |\n| Moving stop into the zone | Giving the trade \"room\" is not a plan | If invalidated, exit. Don't hope. |\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Confluence Checklist (More = Better)\n\nBefore entering, count how many of these align:\n\n- [ ] 4H structure confirms your direction\n- [ ] Fib golden zone (0.5–0.705) aligns with a prior S/R level\n- [ ] Fib zone aligns with a round number ($3,750, $3,800, etc.)\n- [ ] Fib zone aligns with a session high or low (e.g. Asian session high)\n- [ ] Trigger candle appears at the zone\n- [ ] Volume or momentum slows as price enters the zone\n\n> ✅ **3 or more confluences = high probability setup**\n> ❌ **1 confluence = skip it**\n\n---\n\n## 📏 Position Sizing Reminder\n\nNever risk more than **1–2% of your account** per trade regardless of how strong the setup looks. Use the Position Size Calculator in the app Settings to calculate your lot size based on:\n- Your account balance\n- Your stop loss distance in pips/dollars\n- Your chosen risk percentage\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Summary\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Context** | 4H bearish or bullish trend confirmed |\n| **Location** | 0.5–0.705 Fibonacci golden zone on 1H impulse |\n| **Scenario** | IF price retraces into zone AND shows rejection THEN continue in trend direction |\n| **Trigger** | Bearish/bullish engulfing, sweep + reversal, or 5m structure break |\n| **Invalidation** | Close beyond 0.786 or beyond the impulse origin |\n| **Target** | Prior swing low/high — minimum 2R |",
