    "id": "lesson-2qGrZC",
    "moduleId": "strategies",
    "title": "Strategy 3: Liquidity Sweep & Reversal",
    "summary": "How to identify and trade stop hunts — when smart money pushes price beyond a key level to grab liquidity before reversing hard.",
    "body": "# Strategy 3: Liquidity Sweep & Reversal\n\n> **The Edge:** Retail traders place stops at obvious levels. Smart money pushes price to those levels, triggers the stops (grabbing liquidity), then reverses. You can trade the reversal.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The Core Idea\n\n**Liquidity** in trading means pending orders — stop losses, take profits, and limit orders — clustered at predictable price levels. These levels include:\n\n- Previous day highs and lows\n- Session highs and lows (Asian range high/low)\n- Equal highs or equal lows (double tops/bottoms)\n- Round numbers ($3,700, $3,750, $3,800)\n- Recent swing highs and lows\n\nSmart money (banks, institutions, large funds) needs **large order flow** to fill their positions. They deliberately push price into these clusters, trigger the retail stops, and use that liquidity to fill their own orders — then reverse price hard in the opposite direction.\n\nThis is known as a **liquidity sweep**, a **stop hunt**, or a **stop raid**.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 What a Liquidity Sweep Looks Like\n\nOn the chart, a liquidity sweep appears as:\n\n- A **sharp spike** beyond a key high or low\n- Often a **long wick** on a candle (price went there but closed back away from it)\n- Followed by an **aggressive reversal** in the opposite direction\n- The move beyond the level is usually **fast and impulsive** — not slow and grinding\n\n> 📌 Example: Gold has been holding above $3,720 for hours. Everyone's stops are just below $3,720. Price suddenly dips to $3,715, triggers all those stops, then **rockets back above $3,720** and never looks back.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Step-by-Step Process\n\n### Step 1 — Identify the Liquidity Pool\nLook for areas where retail stop orders are likely clustered:\n- **Equal highs or equal lows** (price touched the same level twice = stops stacked there)\n- **Previous day high (PDH) or previous day low (PDL)**\n- **Asian session high or low** (especially powerful at London open)\n- **Round numbers** that have been tested before\n- **Recent swing high/low** that everyone can see on the chart\n\n### Step 2 — Wait for the Sweep\nDo NOT anticipate the sweep — wait for it to actually happen:\n- Price moves **beyond** the liquidity level (breaks the high or low)\n- The move is **fast** — one or two candles spike through\n- A **long wick** forms on the 5m or 15m candle that pierced the level\n- Price then **closes back** on the other side of the level\n\n### Step 3 — Look for Confirmation of Reversal\nAfter the sweep, watch for:\n- 🕯️ A **strong reversal candle** closing back through the swept level\n- 🔄 A **break of short-term structure** in the reversal direction (on 5m)\n- 📉 If bearish sweep: price sweeps above highs → then breaks below a recent low on 5m\n- 📈 If bullish sweep: price sweeps below lows → then breaks above a recent high on 5m\n\n### Step 4 — Entry\n- Enter **after** the reversal candle closes (confirmation entry)\n- OR enter on the **retest** of the swept level from the other side (more conservative)\n- Do NOT enter on the wick itself — wait for the close\n\n### Step 5 — Invalidation\n- If price **continues beyond** the swept level (doesn't reverse), the sweep thesis is wrong\n- Stop goes just beyond the extreme wick of the sweep\n- Keep stops **tight** — if it was a real sweep, price should not go back there\n\n### Step 6 — Target\n- **First target:** The next structural level in the reversal direction\n- **Second target:** The origin of the move that created the liquidity pool\n- Aim for minimum **2R** — sweeps that work tend to move fast and far\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Two Types of Sweeps\n\n### Bearish Sweep (Sell the Sweep of Highs)\n- Price pushes **above** equal highs or a key high\n- Retail longs get trapped; short sellers get stopped out\n- Smart money fills sell orders using this liquidity\n- Price reverses **sharply downward**\n- You **sell** after the sweep and reversal confirmation\n\n### Bullish Sweep (Buy the Sweep of Lows)\n- Price pushes **below** equal lows or a key low\n- Retail shorts get trapped; long buyers get stopped out\n- Smart money fills buy orders using this liquidity\n- Price reverses **sharply upward**\n- You **buy** after the sweep and reversal confirmation\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 High-Probability Sweep Checklist\n\n- [ ] Clear liquidity pool visible on the chart (equal highs/lows, PDH/PDL, round number)\n- [ ] HTF (4H) bias aligns with the reversal direction\n- [ ] Sweep happened during an active session (London or NY — NOT Asia)\n- [ ] Wick clearly pierced the level but candle closed back through it\n- [ ] 5m structure broke in the reversal direction after the sweep\n- [ ] Next structural target is at least 2R away\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| Anticipating the sweep before it happens | Wait — let the wick form and price close back first |\n| Trading sweeps against the 4H trend | Only trade sweeps that align with HTF direction |\n| Entering on the wick, not the close | Always wait for the candle to close before entering |\n| Setting stop too far away | Stop goes just beyond the wick extreme — keep it tight |\n| Confusing a breakout with a sweep | A sweep has a fast wick and closes back; a breakout has a strong body close |\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Summary\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Context** | 4H bias clear; obvious liquidity pool visible on chart |\n| **Location** | Equal highs/lows, PDH/PDL, Asian range high/low, round numbers |\n| **Scenario** | IF price sweeps beyond the liquidity level AND closes back through it AND breaks 5m structure THEN reversal trade in opposite direction |\n| **Trigger** | Reversal candle close + 5m structural break |\n| **Invalidation** | Price continues beyond the sweep extreme (no close-back) |\n| **Target** | Next structure level — minimum 2R |",
