    "id": "lesson-Md3Vpz",
    "moduleId": "strategies",
    "title": "Strategy 4: Session Open Play",
    "summary": "How to trade the directional expansion that follows the quiet Asian session — one of the most consistent and time-based setups in gold trading.",
    "body": "# Strategy 4: Session Open Play\n\n> **The Edge:** The Asian session sets a quiet range. London and New York sessions are where the real moves happen. Trading the breakout of the Asian range at session open is one of the most time-consistent setups in gold.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The Core Idea\n\nGold trading is heavily influenced by **which session is active**. The three major sessions — Asia, London, and New York — have very different personalities:\n\n| Session | Time (GMT) | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Asia** | 00:00 – 08:00 | Quiet, low volatility, range-bound |\n| **London** | 08:00 – 16:00 | High volatility, trend initiation, big moves |\n| **New York** | 13:00 – 21:00 | High volatility, continuation or reversal of London move |\n| **Overlap** | 13:00 – 16:00 | Highest volatility of the day |\n\nThe **Asian session** typically creates a **consolidation range** — a defined high and low. When London opens at 08:00 GMT, institutional traders begin their day and inject massive volume into the market. This volume **breaks the Asian range** and creates the directional move of the day.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Step-by-Step Process\n\n### Step 1 — Define the Asian Range\n- Mark the **Asian session high** and **Asian session low** on your chart (00:00–08:00 GMT)\n- Use a rectangle or horizontal lines to clearly box this range\n- The range is typically **$5–$20 wide** on gold depending on market conditions\n- A **tighter Asian range = more explosive London breakout** (compression = expansion)\n\n### Step 2 — Check the 4H Bias\n- Before London opens, check the **4H chart** for directional bias\n- Is price in a bullish structure (HH/HL) or bearish structure (LH/LL)?\n- This tells you which side of the Asian range to **favour**\n- Bullish bias = favour the upside breakout\n- Bearish bias = favour the downside breakout\n\n### Step 3 — Wait for London Open (08:00 GMT)\n- At 08:00 GMT, volume increases sharply\n- Watch for price to **break above the Asian high** or **break below the Asian low**\n- The breakout should be a **strong, fast move** — not a slow grind\n\n### Step 4 — Entry Options\n\n**Option A — Aggressive Entry (Breakout)**\n- Enter immediately on the **breakout candle close** beyond the Asian high/low\n- Faster entry, but higher risk of false breakout\n- Best when 4H bias is very clear and the Asian range is tight\n\n**Option B — Conservative Entry (Retest)**\n- Wait for price to break the Asian high/low, then **pull back and retest** the broken level\n- Enter on rejection at the retest\n- Lower risk, but you may miss the move if it runs immediately\n\n### Step 5 — Invalidation\n- If you entered on the **upside breakout**: stop goes below the Asian session low\n- If you entered on the **downside breakout**: stop goes above the Asian session high\n- If price sweeps the breakout and returns inside the Asian range — exit immediately\n\n### Step 6 — Target\n- **First target:** 1× the height of the Asian range projected from the breakout point\n- **Second target:** Next major structure level in the breakout direction\n- Aim for minimum **2R**\n\n---\n\n## 📊 New York Session Open Play\n\nThe same concept applies at **New York open (13:00 GMT)**:\n- Mark the **London session high and low** (08:00–13:00 GMT)\n- At 13:00 GMT, New York opens and often breaks the London range\n- Particularly powerful when London has been consolidating before NY\n\n> ⚡ The **London/NY overlap (13:00–16:00 GMT)** is the highest-volume period of the day — the biggest gold moves often happen here.\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 High-Probability Session Open Checklist\n\n- [ ] Asian range clearly defined (tight range = better)\n- [ ] 4H bias confirmed in the breakout direction\n- [ ] Breakout happens within the **first 30–60 minutes** of session open\n- [ ] Breakout candle is strong (large body, minimal wick on breakout side)\n- [ ] No major news event within 30 minutes of planned entry (check calendar)\n- [ ] Target level at least 2R away\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| Trading the Asian range itself | Asia is for planning, London is for trading |\n| Taking every breakout regardless of bias | Only trade in the 4H trend direction |\n| Entering too early before the session opens | Wait for 08:00 GMT — volume must confirm |\n| Ignoring the news calendar | A high-impact news event can invalidate the setup entirely |\n| Holding through major news if in a trade | Reduce size or close before the event |\n\n---\n\n## ⏰ Key Times to Have on Your Radar\n\n| Time (GMT) | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 08:00 | London open — Asian range breakout |\n| 10:00 | Mid-London — momentum check |\n| 13:00 | New York open — London range breakout or continuation |\n| 13:30 | US economic data releases (NFP, CPI, etc.) |\n| 15:00–16:00 | London close — possible reversal or continuation |\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Summary\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Context** | 4H bias clear; Asian range defined and tight |\n| **Location** | Asian session high or low (key breakout levels) |\n| **Scenario** | IF London opens AND breaks Asian high/low in direction of 4H bias THEN trade the breakout or retest |\n| **Trigger** | Breakout candle close beyond Asian range; or rejection at retest of broken level |\n| **Invalidation** | Price returns inside the Asian range after breakout |\n| **Target** | 1× Asian range height projected from breakout; or next structure level — minimum 2R |",
