    "id": "lesson-8YIGvR",
    "moduleId": "strategies",
    "title": "Strategy 12: Inside Bar Breakout",
    "summary": "How to trade inside bars on gold — tight consolidation candles that signal a coiling of energy before a sharp directional move.",
    "body": "# Strategy 12: Inside Bar Breakout\n\n## The Core Idea\nAn **inside bar** is a candle (or series of candles) whose **high and low are completely contained within the previous candle's range**. It signals a pause — the market is coiling, compressing energy before its next move. When price breaks out of the inside bar's range, it often does so **sharply and with conviction**.\n\n> Think of it like a spring being compressed. The tighter the compression, the more explosive the release.\n\n---\n\n## What Is an Inside Bar?\n\n### Definition\n- The **mother bar** = the large candle before the inside bar\n- The **inside bar** = a smaller candle whose high is lower than the mother bar's high AND whose low is higher than the mother bar's low\n- Multiple consecutive inside bars = even more compression = potentially more explosive breakout\n\n```\nMother Bar     Inside Bar\n|              \n|   ← high     |  ← inside bar high (lower than mother)\n|              |\n|              |  ← inside bar low (higher than mother)\n|   ← low\n```\n\n---\n\n## The Professional Process\n\n### Step 1 — Context\n- Inside bars are most powerful when they form **after a strong impulse move** (the mother bar is large and directional)\n- They are also powerful at **key HTF levels** — the pause signals indecision at a decision point\n- Avoid inside bars in **choppy, ranging markets** — they produce many false breakouts\n\n### Step 2 — Location\n- Look for inside bars forming at:\n  - Key support or resistance zones\n  - After a strong impulse (continuation play)\n  - At session highs/lows (Asian range boundary)\n  - Near round numbers on gold (e.g. $3,700, $3,750, $3,800)\n\n### Step 3 — Scenario\n> *\"IF a strong bullish impulse forms and then an inside bar appears at a key resistance level, AND price breaks above the inside bar's high, THEN I enter long targeting the next key level.\"*\n\n### Step 4 — Trigger\nTwo approaches:\n1. **Breakout entry:** Place a buy stop above the inside bar's high (or sell stop below its low) — entered automatically when price breaks out\n2. **Wait and confirm:** Watch for a strong candle to close above/below the inside bar's range before entering\n\n### Step 5 — Invalidation\n- Stop goes **below the inside bar's low** (for a long) or **above the inside bar's high** (for a short)\n- If price breaks the opposite side of the inside bar → false breakout, exit\n\n### Step 6 — Target\n- First target: next key structure level or swing high/low\n- Measured move: project the mother bar's range from the breakout point\n\n---\n\n## Inside Bar Variations\n| Type | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Single inside bar** | One candle contained within the mother bar — standard setup |\n| **Multiple inside bars** | 2–4 consecutive inside bars — tighter compression, bigger potential move |\n| **Inside bar at key level** | Most powerful — compression at a decision point |\n| **Inside bar after impulse** | Continuation play — pause before the next leg |\n\n---\n\n## Gold-Specific Notes\n- Inside bars on the **1H and 4H** are most reliable for gold day trading\n- The **Asian session** frequently produces inside bars (low volatility) that break out during **London open** — this is a high-probability setup\n- Gold's spread during low-liquidity periods can trigger false breakouts on very small inside bars — stick to bars with meaningful range\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n- ❌ **Trading inside bars in choppy markets** — you need a clear directional context\n- ❌ **Inside bar is too small** — micro inside bars in low liquidity are noise, not signals\n- ❌ **Not waiting for the breakout** — entering mid-inside bar is just guessing direction\n- ❌ **Ignoring the mother bar** — a small, weak mother bar makes the inside bar less meaningful\n\n---\n\n## Quick Checklist\n- [ ] Clear mother bar identified (ideally strong and directional)\n- [ ] Inside bar fully contained within mother bar's range\n- [ ] HTF bias identified — trade breakout in the trend direction where possible\n- [ ] Location: inside bar at a key level or after a strong impulse\n- [ ] Entry: breakout above high (long) or below low (short)\n- [ ] Stop: below inside bar low (long) or above inside bar high (short)\n- [ ] Target: next key level or measured move\n- [ ] Minimum 2R confirmed before entry",
